#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67ca6dfb-d6af-559c-82bf-ebef070247b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359371} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7e3d5f-ce3f-53a2-8ca5-49d857f63430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359372} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a3ad7f-7674-54d8-8241-16b0a9d49b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359373} {:answer/id #uuid "9360bdf0-1ac6-5f4f-8bb4-9a6cd43a5b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+3{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359374} {:answer/id #uuid "440cc302-7062-5eb8-8252-a123c74e21eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67ca6dfb-d6af-559c-82bf-ebef070247b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359371} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e425e93-f692-5345-8f23-5bdc03925a49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da628d4c-a325-5343-bcd6-7344b3c8f181" :question/math-academy-id "q-95114" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nAssume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDomain clarification: The problem assumes $x>0$, so $\\sqrt{x^2}=x$ in the source derivation above. Without that assumption, $f(x,x^3)=|x|\\sqrt{1+x^4}$ and\n$$\\frac{df}{dx}=\\operatorname{sgn}(x)\\frac{1+3x^4}{\\sqrt{1+x^4}}\\quad(x\\ne0).$$\nFor $x<0$ the derivative is the negative of the displayed answer. At $x=0$, the one-sided difference quotients tend to $-1$ and $1$, so the derivative does not exist. The original unqualified prompt and worked solution remain preserved in the source capture and database history."}]}
